-- title:
--   Stabilising the exceptional valuation iff fixing the supersingular point (q=2)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime subject to $q=2$, and $M'$ is a nonzero natural number with $q\nmid M'$. A further prime $\ell_g$ is given with $\ell_g\equiv 11\pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g\mid M'$.
--
--   **Reduction data.** $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}=\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\ \mathbb Q$ lying over $q$ in the sense of `LiesOverPrime`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A$. $W$ is a finite set of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$ over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$ whose members are (hypothesis `hW`) exactly the supersingular places $\mathrm{ssPlaces}\ q\ M'$: those places $w$ that are rational, are affine geometric places, and whose value at the geometric generator $j$ lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\ q$, i.e. is a $j$-invariant all of whose elliptic curves have trivial $q$-torsion. The hypothesis `hle` asserts the inclusion $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ M'\le \mathrm{fieldBar}\ q\ M'$ of subfields of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, the first being the base change of the full level-$M'$ modular function field, the second the base change of the $X_H$ function field of level $q^2M'$ for $H=\mathrm{levelH}\ q\ M'$. $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ M'$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$: a valuation subring $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ of the source, a surjective residue homomorphism onto the target with kernel the maximal ideal, a map on places preserving degrees, the condition that a constant lies in the integers exactly when it lies in $A$ and reduces to its residue, the scaling condition that every nonzero element becomes an integer with nonzero residue after multiplication by a constant, and compatibility of orders with the map on places. The hypothesis `hR₀` says that this residue computes coefficientwise reduction: for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ M'$, that element lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$. Finally $s$ is an element of $W$, that is, a supersingular place.
--
--   **Tame constant field.** $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\mathbb Q$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and $\pi_0\in k_0$ has image in $A$. Writing $A\cap k_0$ for the contraction $A.\mathrm{comap}$ of $A$ along $k_0\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$, the hypotheses require $A\cap k_0$ to be a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, Henselian, with algebraically closed residue field, and (hypothesis `hκ`) that every $a\in A$ differs by an element of the maximal ideal of $A$ from some $c\in k_0$ lying in $A$.
--
--   **Auxiliary primes, roots of unity, uniformisers.** $\ell$ is a prime with $\ell\ge 3$, $\ell\ne q$ and $\ell\nmid M'$; $\zeta_0\in k_0$ is a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; $\varpi_t\in k_0$ lies in $A$ and satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=q\cdot u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$. Further, $K_b$ is an intermediate field of $k_0$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $K_b=\bot$, $A_b$ is a valuation subring of $K_b$ consisting exactly of the elements whose image lies in $A$, and $\varpi_b$ is a nonzero generator of the maximal ideal of $A_b$. The tameness hypothesis `htame` asserts that the natural number $(\ell_g-1)/2$ is a unit of $A\cap k_0$, and $\xi\in k_0$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity.
--
--   **Level structure and the function field.** $H_1$ is the subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ equal to $\mathrm{levelH}\ q\ M'$ intersected with the kernel of the reduction of unit groups to modulus $\ell_g$ (available because $\ell_g\mid M'$ and hence $\ell_g\mid q^2M'$); $\mathrm{levelH}\ q\ M'$ is itself the kernel of a reduction map of unit groups from the modulus $q^2M'$. $K_\ell$ is the intermediate field of $k_0$ in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ k_0$ given by $\mathrm{laurentBaseChange}\ k_0$ of $\mathrm{xHFunctionField}\ (q^2M')\ H_1$, i.e. the subfield generated over $k_0$ by the coefficientwise images of that function field over $\mathbb Q$; it carries an $A\cap k_0$-algebra structure compatible with $k_0$. The element $j_\ell\in K_\ell$ is the one whose Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion $\mathrm{jq}$ of $j$, and it is nonzero. The element $\varpi_t'\in A\cap k_0$ maps to $\varpi_t$.
--
--   For $\gamma\in SL_2(\mathbb Z)$, an automorphism $\tau\in K_\ell\simeq_{k_0}K_\ell$ is called *a level automorphism attached to $\gamma^{-1}$* when $\mathrm{IsLevelAutAt}\ k_0\ q\ \xi\ q\ (q^2M')\ H_1\ \gamma^{-1}\ K_\ell\ \tau$ holds: for every weight $k$, every two modular forms $f,g$ of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$ and $p_g$ of nonzero associated series, every $x\in K_\ell$ whose Laurent series is the image of the ratio of those series, and every ring embedding $\iota:k_0\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi)=e^{2\pi i/q}$, the $\iota$-coefficientwise image of the expansion of $\tau x$ multiplied by the $q$-expansion of $g\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\ q\ \gamma^{-1}$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\ q\ \gamma^{-1}$, where $\mathrm{conjElemN}\ q\ \gamma$ is the matrix obtained from $\gamma$ by dividing the upper right entry by $q$ and multiplying the lower left entry by $q$.
--
--   **The chart and the point.** $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\ (A\cap k_0)\ K_\ell\ j_\ell$ is the subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $(A\cap k_0)[j_\ell]$, with distinguished element $\mathrm{jChartFin}=j_\ell$. A maximal ideal $y$ of this chart algebra is given, containing the image of $\pi_0$ (`hϖy`), and supersingular (`hss`): for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel exactly $y$, $\varphi(j_\ell)\in\mathrm{ssJSet}\ q\ \Omega$.
--
--   Call a maximal ideal $y'$ of the chart algebra *centred on $s$* when: for every $g\in\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFull}\ M'$ whose base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$, such that $\mathrm{ord}_P(g)\ge 0$ at every place $P$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ M'$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ at which $\mathrm{ord}_P(j)\ge0$, and such that the $R_0$-residue of $g$ lies in the valuation subring of $s$, whenever the coefficientwise image of $g$ lies in $K_\ell$ and in the chart algebra, then for every $c\in k_0$ lying in $A$ whose residue in $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$ equals the value at $s$ of the $R_0$-residue of $g$, the chart element $g-c$ lies in $y'$. The hypothesis `hover` asserts that $y$ is centred on $s$, and `hOrbit` asserts transitivity: for every maximal ideal $y'$ of the chart algebra containing the image of $\pi_0$ and centred on $s$, there exist $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ which maps the chart algebra into itself and satisfies $b\in y\iff \tau b\in y'$ for chart elements $b$.
--
--   **The fibre data.** $B_t$ is an $A\cap k_0$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$, $W_t$ a valuation subring of $K_\ell$, and $B_t\subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`). The hypothesis `hS1` is a conjunction requiring: the chart algebra is contained in $B_t$; $K_\ell$ is the fraction field of $B_t$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A\cap k_0$; $B_t/(\pi_0)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; $W_t$ meets $k_0$ exactly in $A\cap k_0$, is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by the image of $\pi_0$, and is the localisation of $B_t$ at that maximal ideal; $y$ is the contraction to the chart algebra of the maximal ideal of $W_t$; further, for every $\mathbb F_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $\kappa$ of $A\cap k_0$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho$ from $B_t$ onto the Drinfeld coordinate ring $\mathrm{CoordRing}\ q\ \kappa$ (two-variable polynomials modulo the Drinfeld polynomial minus one) whose vanishing locus is the maximal ideal of $W_t$, which is compatible with reduction on constants, and which is equivariant in the following sense: for every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and every level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ there is $c\in\mathbb F_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\ q\ \gamma,c)$ in $\mathrm{hSubgroup}\ q$ (the kernel of $(g,c)\mapsto \det(g)\,c^{q+1}$) such that $\rho\circ\tau=\mathrm{hAction}\,(\mathrm{redQ}\ q\ \gamma,c)\circ\rho$ on $B_t$, with $c\ne1$ whenever $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity, and with the scalar $c$ also realised by a pair with trivial matrix part: there exist $\gamma'\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau'$ attached to $\gamma'^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ with $(1,c)\in\mathrm{hSubgroup}\ q$ and $\rho\circ\tau'=\mathrm{hAction}\,(1,c)\circ\rho$ on $B_t$ (the linked form of the scalar clause). The conjunction continues: level automorphisms attached to elements of $\Gamma_0(M')$ preserve $B_t$; every prime ideal $Q$ of $B_t$ containing the image of $\pi_0$ is reached from the maximal ideal of $W_t$ by some level automorphism attached to an element of $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$; and an element $b\in B_t$ all of whose $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$-translates lie in the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is divisible by the image of $\pi_0$.
--
--   **Ends and the rotation data.** $n\ge1$ divides $q+1$; $\gamma_0\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau_0$ is a level automorphism attached to $\gamma_0^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$; $m\ge1$ satisfies $\pi_0^m=\varpi_t'\cdot(\text{unit})$ in $A\cap k_0$; $\zeta_c$ is an element of the adic completion of $A\cap k_0$ with $\zeta_c^{\,n}=1$ and $\zeta_c^{\,k}-1$ a unit for $0<k<n$; and $\mathrm{ends}$ is a finite set of subrings of $K_\ell$. The hypothesis `hR7` is a conjunction (summarised here) requiring: $\tau_0^n$ acts trivially on $B_t$ while $\tau_0^k$ does not for $0<k<n$; every level automorphism attached to an element of $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with some $\tau_0^k$, $k<n$; $\mathrm{ends}$ has exactly $q+1$ members; level automorphisms attached to elements of $\Gamma_0(M')$ and preserving $W_t$ permute $\mathrm{ends}$, and act transitively on it; each member of $\mathrm{ends}$ is $\tau_0$-stable; distinct members are separated by an element that is a non-unit in the one and a unit in the other; each $O\in\mathrm{ends}$ is a Noetherian local ring contained in $W_t$, contains the chart algebra, meets $k_0$ exactly in $A\cap k_0$, has every element congruent to a constant modulo non-units, and admits an isomorphism $\iota$ of its adic completion with the crossing model $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}$ over the completion of $A\cap k_0$ with parameter $\pi_0^m$, together with elements $c_x,c_y\in O$ with $c_xc_y=\pi_0^m\cdot(\text{unit})$ matching the two coordinates $U,V$ up to units, $c_y$ in and $c_x$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_t$, and an automorphism $\theta_0$ of the crossing model conjugating $\tau_0$, acting on $U$ and $V$ by $\zeta_c$ and its inverse modulo the square of the maximal ideal; in addition, for each such $O$ there is a valuation subring $W_x$ of $K_\ell$ containing $O$ and the chart algebra, meeting $k_0$ in $A\cap k_0$, a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, carrying an element $t$ no nonzero polynomial over $A\cap k_0$ with some unit coefficient vanishing at it modulo the maximal ideal, with $c_x$ in and $c_y$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_x$, whose contraction to the chart algebra is contained in $y$ and strictly smaller than $y$; and a finite-type $A\cap k_0$-subalgebra $B_x\subseteq B_t\cap O$, stable under level automorphisms attached to elements of $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, whose elements are regular at every place of $K_\ell$ over $k_0$ at which $j_\ell$ is regular, and with $O$ the localisation of $B_x$ at the elements that are units in $O$. The last clause of `hR7` is a dichotomy: every valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ meeting $k_0$ exactly in $A\cap k_0$, containing the chart algebra and with $y$ contracted into its maximal ideal, either contains $B_t$, or contains some $O\in\mathrm{ends}$ in such a way that the non-units of $O$ land in the maximal ideal of $V$.
--
--   **One direction assumed.** The hypothesis `hR5d` states that for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and every level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma^{-1}$, if $b\in y\iff\tau b\in y$ for all chart elements $b$ with $\tau b$ again in the chart algebra, then $f\in W_t\iff \tau f\in W_t$ for all $f\in K_\ell$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $k_0$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K_\ell$ for which there exists $\gamma\in SL_2(\mathbb Z)$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$, $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\mathrm{IsLevelAutAt}\ k_0\ q\ \xi\ q\ (q^2M')\ H_1\ \gamma^{-1}\ K_\ell\ \tau$, the following two statements are equivalent:
--
--   (i) for every $f\in K_\ell$, $f\in W_t$ if and only if $\tau f\in W_t$;
--
--   (ii) for every element $b$ of $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\ (A\cap k_0)\ K_\ell\ j_\ell$ such that $\tau b$ again lies in that chart algebra, $b\in y$ if and only if $\tau b\in y$.
--
--   This is the rigidity step in the local analysis of a supersingular point of the regular model: among the level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, those stabilising the exceptional valuation $W_t$ of the fibre are exactly those fixing the closed point $y$ of the integral chart, here in the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$ frame with linked scalars and at the small prime $q=2$. It feeds the count of the decomposition order at such a point, [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.two_mul_rigidChart_decompositionOrder_eq_natCard_rationalAut_of_moduliPlace_of_decompositionUnique_linkedScalars_of_eq_two_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.two_mul_rigidChart_decompositionOrder_eq_natCard_rationalAut_of_moduliPlace_of_decompositionUnique_linkedScalars_of_eq_two_of_dvd).
